<blockquote>
Guild Evolution: Strife!<br />
Written by <a href="mailto:mayhem@conclaveofguilds.com">Mayhem</a><br />
<br />
This is a series of articles based on the growth and changes which take
place in guilds.  Unlike the rest of our articles, this one is not based
upon a guild being formed prior to the release of a game.  This article
topic actually comes from an email I received shortly after Conclave of
Guilds opened.  The author of the email forwarded me a link to a couple of
conversation logs wherein a former Guild Leader was questioned about his
prior poor conduct.  (As an aside, this individual is apparently planning to
play in Everquest II.)  Thus the topic at hand is this: what do you do when
your guild is torn up from within?  After all, this can happen at any time,
from now until the guild doesn't have a game to play in anymore.<br />
<br />
First off, we should be honest and take a look at certain factors.  Not
everyone has the same position within their guild.  How you react to
situations which may arise is dictated as strongly by your status in the
guild as it is your personality.  There's a difference to how you can,
should, and will behave based upon this very important aspect of the
situation.  Remember to bear this in mind while you decide what to do in a
difficult situation like the ones we'll outline later on.<br />
<br />
The other major factor you'll have to remember to take stock of in any kind
of guild conflict is who you are.  Someone who is predisposed to a mercurial
temperament will be more likely to jump into a fray than someone who tends
towards introversion and analysis.  Knowing the strengths and weaknesses of
ourselves is vital in life, not just this situation, so it's valuable to
consider who you are and how you're likely to react to stressful times
within your guild.<br />
<br />
So, these two major factors identified, we'll look at some common (and
not-so-common) situations which areise in a guild, and how you might be able
to deal with them.  First off, let's take the big one which sparked this
article.  Imagine that your Guild Leader is relatively ordinary for a
considerable period of time, then one day just starts to get out of control.
The madness sets in and he begins to alienate other guilds, individuals,
anyone he can, including people within the guild.  He does as many dirty,
nasty and humiliating things as possible, then one day disbands the guild,
leaving behind a bunch of bewildered members wondering what in the world
happened.<br />
<br />
There's a universal law that goes beyond multiplayer game design.  That law
is that some people no matter when or where in life will derive pleasure
from inflicting as much grief on others as they possibly can.  Think about
it for a moment and you'll figure out why.  It is a kind of power to be able
to cause people to react to your actions, and those who seek power are often
leaders within a gaming community.  Sometimes this is due to a feeling of
personal inadequacy, and is fueled by an urge to make up for whatever
personal failings an individual my view themselves as having (however
deep-down) in their real life.  Thus you do get a fair number of asocial
Guild Leaders.  Fortunately this usually manifests in rudeness during raids
(or just in general communication), pushyness with other players, and
general disregard for the rights of others.  This is actually to be
expected, and even the best Guild Leaders have a hard day once in a while.
Unfortunately it can progress to the point at which nobody is safe from the
prolonged lashing out of such an individual.<br />
<br />
As a member of a guild which this is happening to, it is vital that you
communicate with the other members in the guild.  In particular, seek out
the assistance of the officers in trying to defuse the situation.  Don't sit
back and hope that the guild will be there when you've gained another level,
or when this quest is finished.  Be proactive and talk about the problem
with others.  It is the duty of every officer in a guild to try and be of
assistance to the member body, and the duty of every guild member to do what
they can to assist their fellows.  If your guild has a private mailing list
or forum, it's worth getting a discussion opened up about the situation.  As
an officer, it is vital to address the issues, and perhaps the best way to
do this is within a private "officers-only" chat, email, or board for your
guild.  It is always possible that things have not gone so far that the
Guild Leader's behavior cannot be turned around.  If it is reparable, it is
usuallly best if the Guild Leader does not remain in that position after the
crisis has passed.  Another suitable condidate should take over the guild
leader tag.  This of course requires the consent of the present leader, and
thus is only an option if things have been smoothed over relatively well.<br
/>
<br />
If it becomes apparent that there's pretty much nothing to be done regarding
the guild leader, it is wisest to simply rebuild the guild.  Find a strong
leader who is well-liked, and willing to put in the effort from within the
guild, let as many people as possible know about the change in structure,
and re-form the guild under a new name.  Be sure to try and keep as open a
policy as possible about members joining who were in the old guild.  It may
be a considerable time before all of them figure out what is going on.
(People have gone on a vacation and come back to find out that a month
earlier their guild disbanded.)  During this process it is vital that you
keep the other guilds on the server informed of what is happening.
Catastrophic Guild Leader meltdown is not very good for the image of the
people in the guild at the time it happens, and it is a good policy to
remain relatively open about what happened, and put people at ease about the
conduct which may be expected from the (new) guild in the future.<br />
<br />
If none of these things happen, and the guild shatters into multiple guilds,
or is disbanded in a moment of rage, make sure that you take care of
yourself, and try to keep in contact with those guildmates you are closest
to.  Take your time making a decision about guilding again.  There's no
shame in being cautious after such an ordeal.  It may serve you best to take
a bit of time to make some new friends within other guilds.  You may find
the situation is for the best after all.<br />
<br />
The next unhappy occurrence which we'll cover is the meltdown of an officer.
While this may seem much easier than dealing with a Guild Leader, it can be
enough to cause the shattering of what was an otherwise healthy guild.  When
a single guild officer begins to behave in a manner which is disruptive and
damaging, it behooves the rest of the guild's leadership to step in
immediately and try to resolve the situation.  However, as we all know, many
guilds are founded by groups of friends or even relatives, and that counts
for quite a bit, even in the face of seriously asocial behavior.  So what
can you do if the Guild Leader seems content to allow the officer free
rein?<br />
<br />
As a member of the guild, you need to make it clear to the other officers
that you're not happy with the situation.  Point out (with the aid of the
guild charter) how the actions the individual is taking are in violation of
the spirit of the guild.  Talk to the other members and see how they've been
affected by the difficulties, and take a collective case before the officers
and Guild Leader.  It can be a good idea to demonstrate how the individual
is damaging the reputation of the entire guild with their actions.  Often
once the leadership of a guild realizes how serious the situation is when
taken as isolated incidents by multiple individuals outside the guild, there
is a change in the general view of the problem.  Instead of being something
annoying but tolerable, the outbursts can be looked at as seriously damaging
to the image of the group as a whole.  This is often enough to draw some
serious action.<br />
<br />
As an officer in a guild, it is vital to remember that everyone needs to
abide by the terms and spirit of the guild charter.  This means that when
one of your fellows begins to have a breakdown, you need to pay attention to
your duty and step up to deal with it.  Be sure to investigate the events
surrounding the problem.  Try to determine the extent of the damage done,
and be sure to share the information with your fellows.  Between you, there
needs to be action taken, and the Guild Leader must be the one to take it in
most cases.  While possible to censure an officer for bad behavior as a
group of officers, it is only when the Guild Leader has been convinced of
the validity and seriousness of the misbehavior that the officer may be
demoted or deguilded.  Thus it is the duty of the officer corps to impress
upon the Guild Leader the facts, and to offer their suggestions as to an
appropriate resolution.  In some cases this can still be merely censure and
retention of the individual as an officer.  In most other serious cases, it
should involve removal of the errant officer from their position, at the
least.<br />
<br />
So, what about when you're the Guild Leader and an officer gets out of
control?  Place your personal feelings aside.  While being empathic is
valuable in a leader, it doesn't belong in the way when you need to quickly
determine the extent of a problem being caused by one of your "chosen few" .
. . though you would do well to use that empathy (at least as much as you
are fortunate enough to have) once you've determined the facts and need to
decide upon action.  Your first job is to find out the facts.  Talk to guild
members who have witnessed the events, seek out individuals outside the
guild who have been involved, and generally try to get an accurate picture
of what's happened.<br />
<br />
Then you'll need to confront the officer, as tactfully as possible, and
determine what their view on the situation is.  You may find that the entire
issue actually stems from something which is not entirely the fault of the
officer.  In this case, it wouldn't do well to expel the individual from the
guild.  As well, depending on how well the damage can be smoothed out and
what the officer is willing to do in the future, they may well deserve
another chance at staying in their position, after being properly censured
for their actions.  In the vast majority of situations however, the officer
will need to be subjected to disipline, which may include summary dismissal
from the guild itself.<br />
<br />
The final issue, and possibly the easiest to resolve is the breakdown of a
normal member.  As this individual generally is less-integrated than the
Guild Leader or an officer, it can be a rather simple matter to isolate and
deal with.  This is probably the most common of the situations we're
covering, and it is almost guaranteed to happen in a guild (albiet
infrequently), so it bears some thought.<br />
<br />
As a member in a guild that has a rogue individual causing difficulties, it
is imperative that you give the officers and Guild Leader as much
information as you personally have regarding the situation.  You do not
(generally) need to undertake any investigation of the matter beyond your
personal experiences, as this is not your "job", though if asked, you should
aid the guild leadership in doing so.  In addition, if you have a "bond"
(most people are more friendly with certain guildmates than with others)
with the member who is causing strife, it might be a good idea to have a
discussion with them on a personal level about what's going on.  You may
find that there's information you are able to discover which may be of use
in resolving the matter in as smooth and peacful a manner possible.  If this
is the case, you can take your findings to the guild leadership so they may
better understand what is happening on a personal level with the member.
(Do not under any circumstances start sharing things that are private and
which the member may have entrusted you without their explicit
permission!)<br />
<br />
An officer's role in dealing with an explosion within the guild body
involving a single member is quote clear.  The situation needs to be
investigated and dealt with in a swift (and fair) a manner possible.
Checking with guild members who have experienced the situation directly as
well as those individuals outside the guild who may have been adversely
affected is a neccesity.  The better the picture you have of the issues at
hand, the more able you'll be to share informed options with the other
officers and the Guild Leader.  In addition, during this time it is
imperative that you support the individual who is causing the problem.
Support does not mean "take the side of" but rather be available to them,
and actively seek to help them become a force in resolving the outstanding
issues.<br />
<br />
Guild Leaders must remain as fair as possible when members are far out of
line.  Your primary job at this point is to gather the facts (the officers
of the guild should prove of great help in accomplishing this) and then to
resolve the issue efficiently and justly.  Having a personal conversation
with the member at the center of the conflict is often worthwhile, though if
for any reason it is not possible, someone else should be asked to do so and
report back with their findings.  At the end of the day (maybe not
literally) there needs to be a decision made as to how to deal with the
member.  In many cases this may be a deguilding, though other options may
make themselves available.  In some cases, the member will voluntarily leave
the guild, or a situation may be reached wherein it is possible for the
member to make amends and continue within the guild.  Whatever the outcome,
it is vital that it be good for the guild as a whole.  Don't try to hold
onto members when there's too much of a chance that problems will arise in
the future.<br />
<br />
Finally, a few notes on things which apply the the situations above.  Be
sure to keep other guilds and individuals apprised of the relevant public
details regarding any of the situations we've covered in this article.  It
is important that affected guilds and individuals be shown fairness and
courtesy by your guild, and from the newest member to the Guild Leader each
individual needs to ensure that when one of these meltdowns occurs, those on
the outside of the guild are thought of as well.  Without the respect of the
population of the server, you are not much of an organization.
Communication, honesty and respectfulness go a long way towards ensuring
that your guild is looked upon in a positive manner, and will help to ensure
your guild and guildmates are well-treated in turn.<br />
<br />
On the issue of discipline and punishment, it is best if the guild has clear
policies on the procedure for the investigation of incidents, their
resolution, and the punishments which may be enacted as a result.  These
should be detailed in the guild charter, and are vital to the fair and
equitable treatment of members from "great to small" during explosive
incidents.  Charter construction will be covered in August in the Leading
the Masses article, and this issue will (hopefully) be addressed in more
detail at that time.<br />
<br />
In the next issue, we'll go ahead and cover a related topic.  What happens
when there isn't a real explosion of malicious behavior?  What if there is
simply an ideological difference which is causing a rift within the guild?
We'll take a look at why the subtle is even more difficult to deal with then
the overt when we revisit the column in August.<br />
<br />
One last note before I close off this article.  One of the driving goals of
this site is to produce a responsible, mature community of guilds and Guild
Leaders.  If this can be accomplished and a kind of standard organization
can be applied to the various servers, there is a greater chance of dealing
with incidents such as these within Everquest II.  Particularly for major
issues which envelop a whole guild, or even multiple guilds, a centralized
"government" would be of great value.  Such a system only works if the
people who populate the servers, and in particular the leaders of the guilds
are willing to put in the effort to create and maintain the integrity of the
organization.  I am committed to providing the infrastructure which will
allow the success of this on every server, but you as players, guild
members, and leaders will need to support the process and be sure to give
feedback so it becomes something which suits the population as a whole.<br
/>
<br />
</blockquote>

